-- THE CONTROL for `field_variant_offset_conflict.mdk` (#1455 review, finding 1).
--
-- Same shape — a type with TWO named-field constructors, one field selected through a
-- receiver typed by the TYPE and not by a constructor — with ONE variable changed:
-- both constructors place `px` at the SAME offset (0).  The stamp is then immaterial
-- (whichever constructor typecheck picks, the emitted load reads the same slot), so
-- `T-FIELD-VARIANT-CONFLICT` must NOT fire.
--
-- ⚠️ THE ASSERTION IS THE ERROR THAT DOES APPEAR, NOT THE ABSENCE OF ONE.  An "it
-- checks clean" control in this corpus would have to dump the whole 123-line prelude
-- scheme list as its golden (see `impl_method_constraint_ok.mdk`), which is a lot of
-- golden for one bit.  Instead the body is deliberately ill-typed in an UNRELATED way:
-- the annotation says `Bool`, the field is an `Int`.  So the golden is a one-line
-- `Type mismatch`, and it can only be produced if `.px` RESOLVED — the conflict guard
-- returns `None` without a stamp, which would give this file the conflict message
-- instead.  A guard that over-fired would move this golden.
--
-- ⚠️ THIS IS NOT DECORATION.  Without it the conflict fixture is compatible with a much
-- cruder rule — "reject any `.field` on a multi-constructor record type" — which would
-- break real code in this very tree: `frontend/ast.mdk`'s `Decl` shares `pub` between
-- `DInterface` and `DImpl` at offset 0, and `test/references_fixtures/dup_field_def/`
-- selects `t.x` over `data T = | A { x : Int } | B { x : Int }`.  This cell is what
-- distinguishes the rule that shipped from the rule that would have.
data Pair =
  | PA { px : Int, py : Int }
  | PB { px : Int, py : Int, pz : Int }

getPx : Pair -> Bool
getPx p = p.px
